from extras import ex

ex('ecosystem',"A quadrat is a square frame used to count plants in an area. Placing quadrats randomly avoids choosing only the best spots. Multiply the mean number per quadrat to estimate the whole population.",
 [('What do animals often compete for?','Food, water, space and mates.','Light only.','Carbon dioxide.','These resources are limited.'),
  ('Why should quadrats be placed randomly?','To avoid bias in choosing where to sample.','To make the counting faster.','So all quadrats land on plants.','Random placement gives a fair sample.'),
  ('A field is 100 m². Mean daisies per 1 m² quadrat is 4. Estimated daisies in the field?','400.','104.','25.','4 × 100 = 400.'),
  ('What do plants compete for?','Light, water, space and minerals.','Food from animals.','Mates.','Plants make their own food but need these resources.')],
 [('What is an ecosystem?','All the living things in an area and their surroundings.'),
  ('Quadrat counts of buttercups were 3, 5 and 4. The field is 50 m² and each quadrat is 1 m². Estimate the buttercup population.','Mean = (3 + 5 + 4) ÷ 3 = 4 per m².~4 × 50 = 200 buttercups.'),
  ('Explain why weeds can reduce the growth of crop plants.','Weeds compete with the crop for the same resources.~They take light, water, space and minerals.~So less is available for the crop, which grows less.')])

ex('speed',"Speed can be measured in other units, such as km/h or mph. Typical speeds: walking about 1.5 m/s, cycling about 6 m/s, a car on a motorway about 30 m/s. To find the distance, use distance = speed × time.",
 [('A car travels 200 m in 10 s. What is its average speed?','20 m/s.','2000 m/s.','0.05 m/s.','200 ÷ 10 = 20 m/s.'),
  ('Which is a typical walking speed?','1.5 m/s.','15 m/s.','150 m/s.','People walk about 1–2 m/s.'),
  ('A cyclist moves at 5 m/s for 60 s. How far does she travel?','300 m.','12 m.','65 m.','5 × 60 = 300 m.'),
  ('Why is it called average speed?','Speed can change during the journey.','It is always the top speed.','It ignores distance.','It is the overall speed for the whole trip.')],
 [('Write the equation linking speed, distance and time.','Speed = distance ÷ time.'),
  ('A runner covers 400 m in 80 s. Calculate her average speed.','400 ÷ 80.~= 5 m/s.'),
  ('Describe how to measure the average speed of a toy car rolling across the floor.','Measure the distance between two marks with a metre ruler/tape.~Time how long the car takes to travel between the marks with a stopwatch.~Divide distance by time, and repeat to find a mean.')])

ex('dt',"The steeper the line on a distance–time graph, the faster the object is moving. A straight sloping line means a steady speed. Speed = distance ÷ time, so you can read both values from the graph.",
 [('A horizontal line on a distance–time graph shows the object is…','stationary (not moving).','moving at a steady speed.','speeding up.','Distance is not changing.'),
  ('A steeper line means…','a faster speed.','a slower speed.','the object is stopped.','More distance in the same time.'),
  ('A straight line goes from 0 m to 60 m in 12 s. What is the speed?','5 m/s.','720 m/s.','0.2 m/s.','60 ÷ 12 = 5 m/s.'),
  ('A curved line getting steeper shows the object is…','speeding up.','slowing down.','at rest.','Speed increases as the slope increases.')],
 [('What does the gradient (slope) of a distance–time graph show?','The speed.'),
  ('A graph shows 100 m travelled in 20 s, then a flat line for 10 s. Describe the motion.','First it moves at a steady speed of 100 ÷ 20 = 5 m/s.~Then it stops (stationary) for 10 s.'),
  ('Two cyclists’ lines start at the origin; line A is steeper than line B. Explain what this tells you.','Cyclist A covers more distance in the same time.~So A is moving faster than B.~Both are moving at a steady speed because the lines are straight.')])

ex('force',"Balanced forces mean the resultant force is zero: a still object stays still and a moving object keeps the same speed. Unbalanced forces change speed or direction. Force arrows show size and direction.",
 [('Forces of 10 N right and 4 N left act on a box. What is the resultant force?','6 N to the right.','14 N to the right.','6 N to the left.','10 − 4 = 6 N towards the bigger force.'),
  ('If forces on a moving object are balanced, it…','keeps moving at the same speed.','stops immediately.','speeds up.','Zero resultant force means no change in motion.'),
  ('Which is a non-contact force?','Gravity.','Friction.','Air resistance.','Gravity acts at a distance.'),
  ('What is the unit of force?','Newton (N).','Kilogram (kg).','Joule (J).','Forces are measured in newtons.')],
 [('What is the resultant force?','The overall force when all the forces are combined.'),
  ('A rocket has 5000 N thrust upwards and 3000 N weight. Calculate the resultant force and state what happens.','5000 − 3000 = 2000 N upwards.~The rocket speeds up upwards.'),
  ('A car moves at a steady 30 m/s on a straight road. Explain what this tells you about the forces on it.','The car’s speed and direction are not changing.~So the forces are balanced.~The driving force equals the friction and air resistance.')])

ex('gravity',"Weight = mass × gravitational field strength. On Earth, g is about 10 N/kg. On the Moon g is about 1.6 N/kg, so you weigh less but your mass is the same.",
 [('A 50 kg student on Earth (g = 10 N/kg) has a weight of…','500 N.','50 N.','5 N.','W = 50 × 10.'),
  ('On the Moon, your mass would…','stay the same.','decrease.','increase.','Mass is the amount of matter.'),
  ('Weight is measured with…','a newtonmeter (force meter).','a balance in kg.','a ruler.','Weight is a force.'),
  ('Why do you weigh less on the Moon?','The Moon’s gravitational field strength is smaller.','You lose mass in space.','There is no air.','g on the Moon is about 1.6 N/kg.')],
 [('What is weight?','The force of gravity on an object.'),
  ('An astronaut has a mass of 80 kg. Calculate her weight on the Moon, where g = 1.6 N/kg.','W = m × g = 80 × 1.6.~= 128 N.'),
  ('Explain why a bag of sugar has the same mass on Earth and the Moon but a different weight.','Mass is the amount of matter, which does not change.~Weight depends on the gravitational field strength.~The Moon’s field is weaker, so the weight is smaller.')])

ex('crater',"Dropping a ball from a greater height gives it more kinetic energy when it lands. It usually makes a wider, deeper crater. Real meteorite craters are much bigger because meteorites travel very fast.",
 [('In the crater test, what is the independent variable if you change the drop height?','Drop height.','Crater width.','Mass of flour.','You choose the height.'),
  ('Dropping from higher usually makes the crater…','wider.','smaller.','disappear.','The ball has more energy when it lands.'),
  ('Why use the same ball each time?','To keep its mass and size the same (fair test).','To make it bounce.','To change the results.','Changing the ball adds another variable.'),
  ('Why is flour a model for the Moon’s surface?','It is soft and shows marks clearly.','It is made of moon rock.','It is the same colour as space.','A model is simpler and safe to use.')],
 [('What is a model in science?','A simpler version of something real used to test or explain ideas.'),
  ('Give two control variables in the crater experiment.','Same ball (mass/size).~Same depth and type of flour / smoothing the surface the same way each time.'),
  ('Explain why the crater gets bigger when the ball is dropped from higher.','The ball gains more speed as it falls further.~It has more kinetic energy when it hits the flour.~More energy pushes more flour aside, making a bigger crater.')])

ex('elements',"Chemical formulae use small numbers after a symbol to show how many atoms there are, as in CO₂. Some elements exist as molecules, like O₂. There are about 118 known elements.",
 [('What is the symbol for sodium?','Na.','So.','S.','It comes from the Latin name natrium.'),
  ('How many oxygen atoms are in CO₂?','2.','1.','3.','The small 2 is after O.'),
  ('Which is correctly written?','Mg.','MG.','mg.','First letter capital, second lower case.'),
  ('What does the formula NaCl show?','One sodium atom joined to one chlorine atom.','Two sodium atoms.','A mixture of nitrogen and chlorine.','It is sodium chloride.')],
 [('What is the chemical symbol for iron?','Fe.'),
  ('Name the elements in CaCO₃ and how many atoms of each.','Calcium 1, carbon 1.~Oxygen 3.'),
  ('Explain the difference between O₂ and 2O.','O₂ is one molecule of two oxygen atoms joined together.~2O means two separate oxygen atoms.~In O₂ the atoms are chemically bonded; in 2O they are not.')])

ex('polymer',"Natural polymers include cotton, wool and rubber. Synthetic polymers such as polythene are made in factories. Ceramics are hard and heatproof; composites combine materials, like carbon fibre, to get better properties.",
 [('Which is a natural polymer?','Wool.','Polythene.','PVC.','Wool comes from sheep.'),
  ('Why are plastics used for electrical cable covers?','They are good electrical insulators.','They conduct electricity well.','They are magnetic.','Insulators stop current reaching your hand.'),
  ('A problem with many plastics is that they…','do not rot easily and cause litter.','are too heavy.','dissolve in rain.','Most are not biodegradable.'),
  ('Which material is a ceramic?','A china plate.','A plastic bag.','A copper wire.','Ceramics are hard, brittle and heat-resistant.')],
 [('What is a polymer?','A substance with very long molecules made from many repeating units.'),
  ('Give two useful properties of plastics.','Light / flexible / easy to shape.~Waterproof / electrical insulator / strong.'),
  ('Explain why a bike frame might be made from carbon fibre rather than steel.','Carbon fibre is a composite.~It is very strong but much lighter than steel.~A lighter bike needs less energy to ride fast.')])

ex('periodic',"Metals are on the left and in the middle of the periodic table; non-metals are on the right. Elements in the same group react in similar ways. Atomic number tells you the order of the elements.",
 [('Where are most metals found in the periodic table?','On the left and in the middle.','On the far right.','Only in the top row.','Non-metals are on the right.'),
  ('A vertical column is called…','a group.','a period.','a block.','Groups have similar properties.'),
  ('Elements in the same group…','have similar chemical properties.','are always gases.','have the same mass.','They react in similar ways.'),
  ('Which element is a non-metal?','Sulfur.','Sodium.','Iron.','Sulfur is on the right of the table.')],
 [('What is a period in the periodic table?','A horizontal row.'),
  ('Lithium and potassium are in Group 1. Predict one way potassium reacts like lithium.','It reacts with water.~It makes an alkaline solution and hydrogen / fizzes and moves on the surface.'),
  ('Explain how the periodic table helps scientists predict properties of an element.','Elements in the same group have similar properties.~Metals and non-metals are in different areas.~So the position tells you whether it is likely to be a reactive metal, a non-metal, etc.')])

ex('groups',"Group 1 metals get more reactive going down the group. Group 7 halogens get less reactive going down. Group 0 noble gases are unreactive, which is why helium is used in balloons and argon in light bulbs.",
 [('Which Group 1 metal is most reactive?','Potassium (lowest of Li, Na, K).','Lithium.','Sodium.','Reactivity increases down Group 1.'),
  ('Group 1 metals react with water to make…','an alkaline solution and hydrogen.','an acid and oxygen.','salt only.','They are called the alkali metals.'),
  ('Why is helium used in party balloons?','It is less dense than air and unreactive.','It is reactive.','It is coloured.','Noble gases do not burn.'),
  ('Which halogen is most reactive?','Fluorine.','Iodine.','Bromine.','Reactivity decreases down Group 7.')],
 [('What name is given to Group 7 elements?','Halogens.'),
  ('Describe what you would see when sodium is added to water.','It fizzes and floats/moves around on the surface.~It melts into a ball and gets smaller until it disappears.'),
  ('Explain why noble gases are used in light bulbs and balloons.','Noble gases are very unreactive.~They do not react with the hot filament or burn.~This makes them safe to use.')])

ex('transition',"Transition metals often form coloured compounds, such as blue copper sulfate. Many are used as catalysts, such as iron in making ammonia. They are usually hard, dense and have high melting points.",
 [('Which is a transition metal?','Copper.','Sodium.','Calcium.','Copper is in the middle block.'),
  ('Copper sulfate solution is…','blue.','colourless.','orange.','Transition metal compounds are often coloured.'),
  ('Why is copper used for electrical wires?','It is a very good electrical conductor and easy to bend.','It is magnetic.','It is very reactive.','It conducts well and is ductile.'),
  ('Compared with Group 1 metals, transition metals are…','harder and less reactive.','softer and more reactive.','all gases.','They are tough and stable.')],
 [('Give one use of a transition metal.','E.g. iron for buildings/bridges, copper for wires/pipes, gold for jewellery.'),
  ('Give two properties of transition metals.','Hard/strong / high melting point / dense.~Form coloured compounds / good catalysts / conduct electricity.'),
  ('Explain why iron rather than sodium is used to build bridges.','Iron is hard and strong.~Sodium is soft and reacts violently with water.~So a sodium bridge would be weak and dangerous.')])

ex('lungs',"When you breathe in, the intercostal muscles move the ribs up and out and the diaphragm flattens. The chest volume increases, pressure falls, and air rushes in. Alveoli have thin walls, a big surface area and a good blood supply.",
 [('What happens to the diaphragm when you breathe in?','It contracts and flattens.','It relaxes and moves up.','It does not move.','This increases the chest volume.'),
  ('Which gas diffuses from the blood into the alveoli?','Carbon dioxide.','Oxygen.','Nitrogen.','It is then breathed out.'),
  ('Why do alveoli have very thin walls?','To give a short distance for diffusion.','To make them strong.','To stop air escaping.','Gases cross quickly.'),
  ('When you breathe in, the pressure inside your chest…','decreases.','increases.','stays the same.','Bigger volume means lower pressure, so air moves in.')],
 [('What are alveoli?','Tiny air sacs in the lungs where gas exchange happens.'),
  ('Describe what happens to the ribs and diaphragm when you breathe in.','Intercostal muscles contract, moving the ribs up and out.~The diaphragm contracts and flattens.'),
  ('Explain how the structure of the alveoli makes gas exchange efficient.','There are millions of alveoli, giving a large surface area.~Walls are one cell thick, so there is a short diffusion distance.~A good blood supply keeps a high concentration difference.')])

ex('drugs',"Smoking damages the cilia that clean the airways and can cause lung cancer and heart disease. Alcohol slows reactions and can damage the liver. Vapes still contain nicotine, which is addictive and especially harmful for young people.",
 [('Which substance in cigarettes is addictive?','Nicotine.','Tar.','Oxygen.','Nicotine causes dependence.'),
  ('Which disease is strongly linked to smoking?','Lung cancer.','Chickenpox.','Food poisoning.','Tar contains cancer-causing chemicals.'),
  ('Alcohol is a depressant, which means it…','slows down reactions.','speeds up the heart a lot.','improves judgement.','It slows the nervous system.'),
  ('Why are vapes not safe for young people?','Most contain nicotine, which is addictive and affects brain development.','They contain no chemicals.','They clean the lungs.','Vapes are only for helping adults stop smoking.')],
 [('What is a drug?','A chemical that changes the way the body works.'),
  ('Give two harmful effects of smoking.','Lung cancer / heart disease / emphysema.~Damaged cilia, causing coughs and chest infections / lower birth weight in pregnancy.'),
  ('Explain why people find it difficult to stop smoking.','Cigarettes contain nicotine.~Nicotine is addictive, so the body becomes dependent.~Stopping causes withdrawal symptoms, such as cravings and bad mood.')])

ex('nutrition',"Fibre keeps food moving through the gut and water is needed for all chemical reactions. Too little food can cause starvation; too much can cause obesity. A lack of a vitamin or mineral causes a deficiency disease, such as scurvy from too little vitamin C.",
 [('Which nutrient is mainly for growth and repair?','Protein.','Carbohydrate.','Fat.','Protein builds new cells.'),
  ('What does fibre do?','Helps food move through the gut.','Gives lots of energy.','Builds muscles.','It prevents constipation.'),
  ('A lack of vitamin C causes…','scurvy.','rickets.','anaemia.','Scurvy causes bleeding gums.'),
  ('Which food is a good source of carbohydrate?','Pasta.','Chicken.','Butter.','Starch is a carbohydrate.')],
 [('Name the nutrient that provides the most energy per gram.','Fat (lipid).'),
  ('Give two reasons why a diet high in sugar and fat can be harmful.','It can lead to obesity / weight gain.~It increases the risk of heart disease / type 2 diabetes / tooth decay.'),
  ('Explain why an athlete in training needs more carbohydrate and protein than an office worker.','The athlete is more active, so uses more energy.~Carbohydrates release that energy in respiration.~Protein is needed to repair and build muscle tissue.')])

ex('food-tests',"Benedict's solution turns from blue to brick-red when heated with sugar. Biuret solution turns purple with protein. Fats leave a translucent mark on paper or make a cloudy emulsion with ethanol and water.",
 [('Which reagent tests for starch?','Iodine solution.','Biuret solution.','Benedict’s solution.','Iodine turns blue-black with starch.'),
  ('A positive Benedict’s test turns…','brick-red/orange.','blue-black.','purple.','Sugar reacts when heated.'),
  ('Biuret solution turns purple with…','protein.','starch.','fat.','Biuret is the protein test.'),
  ('What must you do in the Benedict’s test?','Heat the mixture in a water bath.','Add iodine.','Freeze the sample.','Benedict’s needs heat to work.')],
 [('What colour does iodine turn if starch is present?','Blue-black.'),
  ('A food turns Biuret purple and iodine stays orange-brown. What does it contain and not contain?','It contains protein.~It does not contain starch.'),
  ('Describe how to test a food for sugar safely.','Mix the food with water and add Benedict’s solution.~Heat in a hot water bath (not a flame) wearing goggles.~A colour change from blue to green/orange/brick-red shows sugar is present.')])
